yield: skin off 3-4 loin steaks — a snack for two
ingredients:
  - item: pork skin
    mass: 150
    basis: true
    note: cut off loin steaks, with whatever backfat came away with it
  - item: fine salt
    mass: 3
    note: 2% of skin weight
  - item: bicarb soda
    mass: 0.4
    optional: true
    note: roughly a quarter of the salt by weight — see notes
  - item: flake salt
    note: to finish, off the heat
components:
  - name: prep
    steps:
      - trim the backfat back to 3-5mm — thicker than that will not render in the time the skin takes to crisp, and you get a crisp shell sitting on a chewy layer
      - cut into 1-2cm strips now, not later — cooked crackling shatters under a knife, and strips render more evenly than a sheet
      - pat bone dry on both faces and along the cut edges
      - rub the fine salt into the skin side, and the bicarb with it if using
      - uncovered on a rack in the fridge for as long as you have — overnight is best, an hour helps, none is survivable. see notes
      - blot off any beaded moisture before it goes near the oven
  - name: render
    steps:
      - gently clamp between two wire racks to keep it flat — skin contracts as it heats and will curl into a tube otherwise
      - clamp over a tray; the fat has to go somewhere
      - oven at 140°C fan-forced, 25-35 mins
      - done when the fat has run and the skin is pale, matte and dry to the touch, with no soft wobble of unrendered fat underneath
      - pour the fat off and keep it — that is lard, and it is the best thing you will ever roast a potato in
      - leave a film on the strips, not a pool
  - name: puff
    steps:
      - oven up to 220°C fan-forced
      - unclamp for the blistered, puffed texture; leave clamped for flat glassy shards. see notes
      - back in for 8-15 mins, watching from 6 — it goes from perfect to acrid in about two minutes
      - out when it is blistered across the whole surface, deep gold, and taps hard and hollow
      - onto a rack, not paper towel — it wants air underneath and it keeps crisping as it cools
      - flake salt now, off the heat, so it stays crunchy instead of dissolving into the fat
  - name: variant (blanched and puffed)
    steps:
      - simmer the skin 45-60 mins until it is soft enough to scrape
      - scrape every trace of fat off the underside with a spoon — this is the whole point of the simmer, and it is tedious
      - dry hard — uncovered in the fridge overnight, or 2-3 hrs in the oven at 80°C fan-forced, until it is translucent, rigid and rattles like plastic
      - blast at 220°C fan-forced, or shallow-fry in the rendered lard at 190°C, until it puffs — 30-90 seconds in oil
    notes: |
      The real chicharrón route, and it produces something the roast route cannot: light,
      hollow, fully puffed, no dense layer anywhere. It also needs the day before, or most
      of an afternoon, and the scraping is genuinely unpleasant work.

      Worth it if you have a lot of skin and want a party snack. Not worth it for the
      trimmings off four steaks.
notes: |
  The day-before question, directly: an overnight uncovered fridge dry is the single
  highest-leverage step in crackling, but on skin this thin the render phase does most of
  the same job, so skipping it costs you less than it would on a belly. Call it 80-85% of
  the result with none of the planning. A whole belly is 20mm+ of fat and skin and the low
  oven cannot dry it and render it at once; a 5mm strip off a loin steak dries in the first
  ten minutes at 140°C anyway.

  Two cheap substitutes if you want most of the difference back on the day. Uncovered in
  the freezer for 30-45 mins — freezers are extremely dry and it pulls surface water fast,
  and thin skin will not freeze solid in that time. Or hold the render at 120°C for the
  first 15 mins before letting it climb, which is just fridge-drying with the door shut.

  The blanch is gone from the default. It was doing the front half of the puffed variant
  below, and stopping there gets you the wet skin without the payoff — you soak the thing
  and then have to drive that water back out, which is precisely the step you were trying
  to avoid. Blanching earns its place when you follow through and scrape the fat off, and
  not otherwise.

  So the two routes are coherent and the middle is not: dry it and roast it, or simmer it,
  scrape it, dehydrate it and puff it. Pick one.

  160°C for the finish crisps by dehydration and gets there eventually, but it is a slower
  and harder-textured result — glassy and tooth-cracking rather than light. The blistering
  comes from water left in the collagen flashing to steam faster than it can escape, and
  that needs real heat. Hence 220°C. Anything hotter risks the fat smoking before the
  centre of a thick strip is through.

  Render low, puff high, in that order, and do not compress the two into one temperature.
  The render is about time at temperature — fat rendering out from under the skin — and the
  puff is about a thermal shock. A single medium heat does both badly.

  Bicarb raises the surface pH, which accelerates Maillard browning and loosens the
  collagen so it puffs more readily. A quarter of the salt weight is the ceiling; past that
  it reads soapy and faintly metallic, and there is no fixing it once it is on. Skip it if
  you are unsure — it is a 10% improvement, not a load-bearing ingredient.

  The clamp is a genuine trade-off. Clamped through both phases gives flat, even, snappable
  shards that stack and dip well. Released before the blast gives curl and bubble and a
  lighter bite, but they twist into awkward shapes. Clamping through the render is not
  optional either way; that is where the curling force is.

  Doneness by look, not clock. Blistered across the entire surface and hard when tapped.
  Any patch still amber and translucent has unrendered fat under it and will be chewy — put
  it back rather than accepting it, because that texture does not improve on cooling. If
  most of a batch is done and a few strips are not, pull the done ones and give the rest
  another five minutes.

  Salt timing is split on purpose. Fine salt before the oven seasons and draws moisture;
  flakes at the end stay as discrete crunchy grains. Flake salt added at the render stage
  just dissolves into the fat and you lose the texture you paid for it.

  It is best within a couple of hours and goes leathery overnight in any humidity. Store it
  in a paper bag rather than an airtight container — sealed, its own residual moisture
  redistributes and softens it. Re-crisp at 200°C fan-forced for 3-4 mins.

  Loin skin is thinner and leaner than belly skin, and less forgiving in one direction and
  more in another: it crisps faster and burns faster, but there is far less fat to render,
  so the whole thing is a 45-minute job rather than a two-hour one.

  Scale by surface area, not weight. Twice the skin at the same thickness is the same
  timings and possibly two trays; twice the thickness is a much longer render.
